const pipelines = [
  ['device-recovery-inspection-pipeline', 'Local recovery evidence inspection and exclusive reservation', ['packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceRecovery.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidDeviceBinding.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidRecoveryRecords.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceRecovery.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/native.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/native.test.ts'], 'The native package exports inspectDeviceLease and CLI exposes native recovery inspect. Inspection reads only the selected device local lease records, without creating storage, acquiring ownership or running platform tools. Reports contain a device digest, evidence fingerprint, local process/record states and fixed issue codes, never tokens, journal bodies, arbitrary saved errors or storage paths. Records remain bounded, no-follow local evidence. Managed Android mutations additionally capture a private parent-owned device-recovery record before writes, binding the stable AVD lease and owner generation to serial, boot and OS digests. Each managed mutation and cleanup rechecks that binding; a detected mismatch permanently fences that binding instance and preserves evidence. Normal lease release refuses any retained device record. Offline inspection exposes deviceRecord and fixed malformed/mismatch issues, and uses its serial digest to correlate app/permission records when selected by avd:<name>. A raw serial selects a different lease key; inspection never performs online discovery. Delegated grants do not contain the parent device recovery path or generation; ordinary native actions and delegated lifecycle identity verification remain unfinished. Android journals additionally require recognized strict app v3 / permission v2 shapes, matching lease key and owner generation, expected device digest, and cross-record package, UID, APK digest and session-install identity; legacy or inconsistent records remain in place and block claim. Permission declarations, desired states and captured baseline sets must agree; verified readback requires matching observations and a justified restored marker. A permission journal missing its installation digest or companion app record reports incomplete-android-installation and cannot claim recovery. Linked/nonregular/oversized/malformed files, invalid owner records, unknown entries and changed directories are reported. The internal claimDeviceRecovery reservation requires exact inspected evidence and rejects live parents/delegates/operations, invalid evidence and existing recovery reservations. Normal acquisition checks the reservation before and after lock creation. Claim work checks original evidence and reservation identity before/after a cancellable total budget, excludes concurrent work and promptly rejects the caller on cancellation while retaining its reservation until actual work settles. Release removes only its own settled reservation and never clears quarantine or original journals. A crashed recovery reservation is not automatically adopted. DeviceLease release refuses retained app as well as permission journals even without an earlier dirty marker, including dangling links. Real Node subprocess tests exercise exited owners, surviving delegates with and without platform operations, crashed recovery holders and preserved original evidence. CLI regression verifies configuration and no platform session creation. Android platform restoration, device readback, durable completion, clearing quarantine, crashed-claim reconciliation and fault-to-restored-device acceptance remain unfinished.'],
  ['runner-recovery-transaction-pipeline', 'Authenticated iOS Runner recovery transaction and durable device exclusion', ['packages/fliwright-native/src/RecoveryTransaction.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/ios/IosRunnerRecovery.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/ios/IosRunnerRecoveryApi.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/RecoveryTransaction.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/IosRunnerRecovery.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/IosRunnerRecovery.crash.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/native.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/native.test.ts'], 'The iOS Runner restorer accepts only an explicit Simulator and current inspection fingerprint, authenticates a fresh challenge, asks the matching Runner generation to exit itself once, and independently reads back Simulator boot generation and Runner absence. RecoveryTransaction appends immutable hash-linked owner epochs, retains the original lease/quarantine bytes, and retires exclusion only after durable readback and verified archival. A pending shutdown is never replayed after a lost response. Atomic publication contention is reported as device_busy; read-only inspection retries transient pending-directory publication changes a bounded number of times. Repeated twelve-way tests prove one claimant wins and eleven are rejected per round. The public restore receipt is scoped to xctest-runner. It does not restore Android permissions, app installs, media or whole-device state. Deterministic SIGKILL boundary tests and one dedicated Simulator crash/restore/new-lease acceptance exist; Worker/Vitest integration, recovery-owner crash coverage, headless/two-device production acceptance and Android recovery remain incomplete.'],
  ['managed-preparation-diagnostics-pipeline', 'Sanitized managed startup failure phases', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/ManagedAppLifecycle.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/ScenarioDevicePreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/ManagedAppLifecycle.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedAppRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/finite.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/InstalledAndroidRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-app.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-native-launch.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-installed-android.test.ts'], 'ManagedAppLifecycle supplies a phase reporter scoped to one preparation request. It accepts only preparation, validate-scenario, build-app, install-app, stop-app, prepare-device, launch-app and verify-endpoint. A failed or timed-out request reports the current fixed phase and retains unknown-outcome and no-replay semantics. The original error reaches only the supervisor onError callback; its message, URL, path and cause are not copied into the public preparation failure. ScenarioDeviceCapabilityError carries the missing requirement and support/readiness state. Only fixed known capability names and unsupported/unverified/not-ready/missing states enter public prepare-device diagnostics; arbitrary names and platform reasons are excluded. Later fenced attempts retain the first sanitized preparation summary without dispatching platform work. iOS basic and Unicode input remain unverified after a timed-out Simulator experiment; adapter-boundary ASCII, size and password validation does not enable that capability. Observer errors do not change failure or cleanup. ManagedAppRun reports frozen scenario validation, stop, device preparation and launch. Finite CLI launch marks VM endpoint verification separately; installed Android launch distinguishes build/install/stop/preparation/launch/verification. Core endpoint normalization and expectation validation also use verify-endpoint. A deadline reports the active phase while unfinished work remains tracked; it does not prove platform work stopped or make a device reusable. Deterministic tests exercise phases, deadline, arbitrary phase rejection, private error retention, failure fencing and installed-path failures. Worker resolver subphases, explicit device recovery, platform hardware acceptance and reliability runs remain separate work.'],
  ['biometric-challenge-observation-pipeline', 'App-local biometric callbacks and observed device event outcomes', ['packages/fliwright-bridge/lib/src/testing/biometric_test.dart', 'packages/fliwright-bridge/test/biometric_test_test.dart', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/BiometricTestController.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/BiometricTestController.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/biometric-fixtures.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/native/NativeConfig.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AdbSession.ts'], 'The opt-in test entrypoint registers FliwrightBiometricTestCapability. Only app-local beginChallenge, recordRejection and completeChallenge methods report platform callbacks; the remote capability exposes reset and getStatus only. Generations fence late callbacks and cleanup. Non-match callbacks increment a rejection sequence while the challenge remains pending, distinct from terminal match/reject/cancel/lockout/error. Core validates bounded status, unique portable IDs, terminal/pending consistency and rejection counts. BiometricTestController serializes observations and counts queue time, status requests, event dispatch and result polling against one cancellable deadline. exerciseChallenge dispatches a caller-supplied platform event once after pending observation and records a separate assertion of the actual app callback. exerciseRejection observes a new non-match sequence with the challenge still pending; a following event can finish that challenge. Lost event responses and missing callbacks fence further dispatch; callbacks receive the remaining timeout and signal. The controller does not call remote methods to fabricate challenges or authentication results. The lazy Vitest biometricTest fixture passes task cancellation, verifies unresolved challenges and resets the matching generation during bounded finalization, preserving body/cleanup errors and failing unresolved skips. Reset clears observations but does not dismiss an OS prompt; owned device cleanup remains necessary. Flutter observation and assertions carry app-test-seam coverage; platform events retain their native metadata. Candidate fingerprint IDs do not prove enrollment: nativeConfig rejects the old baselineVerified boolean, Android emulator fingerprint capabilities remain unverified/not ready and physical injection remains unsupported. iOS injection, real platform challenge/enrollment/lockout recovery and protected business UI verification are still pending. Deterministic core, real Vitest worker and Flutter bridge tests establish the observation contract, not biometric hardware support.'],
  ['android-permission-declaration-pipeline', 'Android scenario permission declarations and independent semantic checks', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/native/AndroidPermissionDeclaration.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/native/AndroidAppReadback.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedAppRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/InstalledAndroidRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/finite.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidPermissionPreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskNativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/native/AndroidPermissionDeclaration.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/native/AndroidAppReadback.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-app.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidPermissionRecovery.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/android-declaration-binding.test.ts'], 'Core decodes a complete camera/microphone declaration, rejects duplicate, unsupported, sparse or malformed entries, and extracts independent snapshots from resolved inherited scenarios. Absent permission layers mean an empty replacement. This pure decoding does not authorize device preparation or change platform support. Managed app control validates its frozen scenario fingerprint and existing release gate before stop, then supplies the validated scene to its preparation callback. The installed Android callback derives the full declaration from that scene instead of hardcoding empty; nonempty high-level scenes are still rejected by the existing gate before build/install/stop. NativeDeviceSession validates and snapshots requests before scheduling platform work, and its underlying permission preparation uses the same decoder. Child binding derives the actual scenario fingerprint and permission declaration itself, checks requested grant and USER_SET/USER_FIXED semantics even if owner and platform agree with each other, and still compares the entire tracked observed set with the supervisor expectation. Live getters recheck scene/declaration changes after asynchronous reads along with installation, process, attempt and Adapter identity. Omitted tracked states remain governed by the original parent baseline; decoding is not proof of prompt UI or effective camera/microphone access. Native regressions prepare inherited camera declarations, repeat an attempt, switch to microphone denial and return to an ordinary peer while retaining durable originals. Real separate Vitest subprocesses import production native sources, acquire delegated leases and independently observe through a bounded deterministic tool executor; they do not run Android hardware or bypass the high-level gate. Direct fixture regressions reject both colluding expectation/observation mismatch with the actual scene and scene mutation during readback. Consumer wrappers pass only request/signal to legacy owner preparation; the resolved scene remains within the installed control callback. Nonempty CLI/Worker scenario release, full retry/skip/unknown-write acceptance and actual Android permission/business validation remain pending.'],
  ['android-permission-recovery-pipeline', 'Durable Android permission baseline and crash exclusion', ['packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidRecovery.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidRecoveryPlatform.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidRecovery.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidPermissionJournal.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidPermissionPreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidPermissionRecovery.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceLease.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceLeaseDelegation.test.ts'], 'The retained parent device lease supplies a private permission-recovery path that is absent from delegated grants. Only that parent may prepare permission baselines or issue managed stop; delegated children retain read-only observation. Before any force-stop, grant, revoke or flag mutation, AndroidPermissionJournal syncs a mode-0600 snapshot containing the isolated UID, original permission states, complete current declaration, desired tracked states and pending step. Original states are captured once and retained when declarations omit or add permissions; a changed baseline UID prevents further preparation or restoration. The record uses a hashed device identity and random journal identity, without serials, APK paths, URLs, credentials or raw commands. First publication is exclusive, never overwriting existing evidence; subsequent commits verify the original file identity, exact bytes, single-link status and enclosing directory through bounded non-following reads, then atomically replace and sync the directory. Checkpoints snapshot caller data before awaiting ownership, reject metadata overrides and malformed/contradictory state, pin the UID and known installation identity, and allow the original baseline only to extend. Lost record ownership fences later preparation and managed stop before any platform work; byte-identical copies and linked/replaced directories cannot be adopted or removed. Successful platform readback must also commit a verified record before the operation can succeed. Persistence failure, cancellation after dispatch and lost replies close mutation and retain quarantine; unknown writes are neither replayed nor blindly restored. Cleanup uses the independent owner deadline and shared device operation mutex, restores original permissions after confirmed stop, completes owned app cleanup, and removes only its own verified-restored record. Lease release refuses remaining recovery evidence, including dangling symlinks, even if no earlier quarantine marker exists. Real Node subprocess regressions write production lease/journal records, are killed after pending or verified-but-not-restored checkpoints, and prove a new owner cannot adopt the lease or erase the baseline. Other tests verify replacement declarations, private mode, write-ahead ordering, persistence failure, cancellation, UID changes and parent/child boundaries. Explicit Android app/permission/forward restoration requires the stopped flag and zero UID processes for every pending or verified action readback, including resumed actions after a real owner SIGKILL. Earlier completed stop checkpoints do not authorize later writes after the app restarts. Before each permission, forward-removal or uninstall dispatch, the platform independently re-observes installation, UID, app-op/permission policy, forward ownership and stopped state within the same recovery deadline. A restart fences later writes and retains the original evidence and quarantine; a restart after dispatch leaves the action outcome unknown without advancing or replaying. Unknown tcp:0 forwarding allocations without a recorded host port remain unqualified for restoration. These are host recovery and deterministic platform-executor tests, not Android device acceptance. Automatic recovery/replay, nonempty high-level permission scenarios and the N5-N8 platform and hardware matrix remain unimplemented.'],
  ['android-owned-build-pipeline', 'Supervised Flutter Android Debug APK construction', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/FlutterAndroidBuild.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/FlutterBuildProcess.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/run.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/flutter-android-build.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidApk.ts'], 'withFlutterAndroidApk is a reusable CLI and Worker-facing build primitive exported by the CLI entry points. It resolves a real Dart entry to its owning Flutter package within the configured project, validates the Android host and build-time argument subset, and never invokes run, install or a device command. A canonical-package host lock serializes concurrent builds across project aliases. Flutter config --machine supplies the actual relative build directory; path escapes and symlink outputs are refused. Build uses build apk --debug with an explicit target and supported flavor/defines/architecture/version settings, without an invented output-dir option. Each Unix command has a detached owned process group, one shared build deadline, combined 4 MiB output cap, Gradle daemon disabling and an independent bounded cleanup interval. Success, failure, cancellation, timeout and output overflow all require leader, pipes and group termination; TERM escalates to KILL. Raw command output and arguments do not enter errors. Atomic synced local checkpoints retain child PID and exclusion when cleanup is uncertain; dead owners are never automatically adopted. The builder requires exactly one fresh regular single Debug APK output, supports a default flavor, refuses ambiguous or stale results, limits bytes to 512 MiB, copies through a non-following file descriptor while checking identity and modification, computes SHA-256 and supplies a mode-0600 private temporary copy to its consumer. The lock releases after staging, allowing installation consumption to overlap a successor build without sharing bytes. Consumer failure also removes staging. Real subprocess regressions cover successful builds, nested packages, argument injection literals, path escapes, default variants, output limits, cancellation, timeout, TERM escalation, surviving descendants, concurrent aliases and retained cleanup ownership. Actual Flutter 3.47.8 host-only construction and the native APK parser passed on a generated Android fixture; no install, device launch, permissions or business acceptance is established. Managed Android emulator CLI and Worker runs now consume this primitive lazily on their first actual attempt through the retained native owner; platform and permission acceptance remain unfinished.'],
  ['android-managed-installed-pipeline', 'Managed Android installed attempts and reset ordering', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/InstalledAndroidRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedNativeRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedAppRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/finite.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerNativeOwnership.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/native-ownership-ipc.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/processes.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-installed-android.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/installed-android-endpoint.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/processes.test.ts'], 'The discovered Android emulator kind selects a retained installed-app backend for native-enabled managed CLI and Worker runs. Trusted project, target, Flutter executable and build arguments remain with the owner; native.tools.apkAnalyzer is required explicitly. A private grant carries only device delegation and authenticated loopback app control, never arbitrary executor commands or artifact paths. Static skips cause no build, install or launch. The first real attempt builds and stages one Debug APK, installs through the retained owner and checks the matching build/install SHA-256. Each attempt then stops the verified installation, checks its read-only device scenario, launches the installed Activity and verifies Bridge capabilities. A separate VM connection observes getVM.pid and the owner rechecks current process binding before returning the endpoint. Retries keep one installation and get distinct generations and endpoints; failures never fall back to flutter run. Keepalive is rejected for this backend. Cleanup closes and awaits control/build/preparation work; unconfirmed build-group cleanup propagates to quarantine. Native quiesce uses an independent bounded cleanup signal, waits platform work, disposes its Adapter, verifies the original artifact before force-stop, removes only owned forwarding, restores permissions and removes only newly installed apps while retaining the device lease. Worker reset runs only after quiescence succeeds, and release follows reset; unresolved or failed quiescence prevents reset/reuse and preserves cleanup causes. Real WebSocket peers verify Bridge and independent PID behavior, a real Node executor consumes the private grant and directly requests consecutive owner endpoints, and deterministic owner/build/reset regressions cover cancellation, no attempts, failures and ordering. These tests do not establish actual Android mobile business acceptance, destructive scenario support or full owner-phase telemetry; Child-side installed APK/UID/PID and expected permission-set readback now precedes every body, with attempt/scenario and managed/launch/Adapter generation binding; ordinary attempts prepare an empty declaration. Nonempty permission scenarios and the platform acceptance matrix remain pending. Physical Android and iOS keep their existing launch backend.'],
  ['android-app-readback-pipeline', 'Independent Android installed-app readback before test bodies', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/native/AndroidAppReadback.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/ManagedAppLifecycle.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidAppObservation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/InstalledAndroidRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskNativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/native/AndroidAppReadback.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/ManagedAppLifecycle.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidAppObservation.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/android-readback-fixtures.test.ts'], 'The installed Android owner retains actual installation SHA-256 and isolated UID, replaces the ordinary attempt permission declaration with empty, and returns launch PID/generation plus expected tracked permission states. Authenticated managed control validates expectation metadata on both sides against the attempt and optional scenario fingerprint; unknown fields, paths, readiness booleans, malformed flags and duplicate permissions are refused. Serialized expectations are not live receipts. Before any body, including ordinary tests without a driver fixture, Vitest reads getVM.pid, verifies current device process binding and independently inspects platform state through its delegated owner. Inspection holds the shared device operation lock with the active Adapter, reads the single installed base APK digest, primary-user isolated UID and exact current main PID, observes requested camera/microphone grants/flags/default app-op, and repeats installation/process identity reads afterward. It rechecks ownership before returning and never writes or captures a restoration baseline. Sequential reads are not an atomic snapshot. The live single-use core handoff checks attempt key/ID, scenario fingerprint, managed/launch/Adapter generations and hashed device identity, snapshots expectations and rechecks identity/cancellation after awaiting the platform. A mismatch prevents the body. Timeline metadata contains only installation/process/permission state and bound generations, without serial, APK paths or authenticated URLs. Every retry obtains a fresh observation; skipped attempts still clean resources. Timeline initialization explicitly depends on the endpoint/attempt fixture so retry recording cannot configure the preceding RunSession after operations. Tests use real Vitest child workers and real lease delegation/fencing, with deterministic tool observations rather than claimed Android device acceptance. Nonempty permission scenarios, platform permission dialogs, biometrics and camera sources remain gated by their separate lifecycle and acceptance requirements.'],
  ['android-installed-app-pipeline', 'Owned Android APK installation and launch without reinstall', ['packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidApk.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidManagedApp.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidAppJournal.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidManagedApp.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceLeaseDelegation.test.ts'], 'The retained native owner exposes low-level installApp and launchApp in managed-start mode on Android emulators. Installation stages a private single APK limited to 512 MiB, hashes it, parses bounded APK-analyzer manifest XML with DTD rejection and validates package, Debug mode, runtime SDK and one exported launcher without a custom process. Existing installations require a matching base APK SHA-256 and isolated primary-user UID; split and differing artifacts are refused without replacement. A new install requires a single-user dedicated emulator, sends one install without replace or grant-all options, records recovery state before dispatch and independently reads back bytes and UID. Installation does not launch. Launch verifies the installed artifact, stopped flag and absence of all UID processes, captures a device log cursor and starts the Activity without flutter run. Only newer logs from the current main PID may provide an authenticated loopback VM endpoint. It allocates and verifies an owned forwarding mapping, rechecks the live PID and returns URL/PID/new generation. The caller must independently verify VM/Bridge and bind the native session. Stop and permission writes guard the same installation identity; final cleanup verifies installation identity and force-stops the owned app before restoring permissions and removing only a newly owned installation. Matching pre-existing apps are kept, changed mappings are not deleted, and new apps are not removed after other users appear. Operations share the device mutex and cancellation/deadline tracking. Parent leases alone provide the private recovery path; cancelled unresponsive installs retain work and exclusion. Unknown mutations are never replayed or blindly restored; quarantine retains a real parent lease recovery record and lock. Version 3 app journals carry random journal and hashed device identities plus lease-key/owner-generation binding, and never include VM authentication URLs. First publication is exclusive; later checkpoints and completion check the original single-link regular file identity, exact bounded bytes and enclosing directory. Normal quiescence completes the verified-restored permission journal after owned app cleanup, then completes only its own closed app journal before releasing the actual device lease. Byte-identical replacement, changed/link records, cancellation and permission completion failure retain evidence and quarantine. Default-lock-directory regressions cover both a new and a pre-existing installation; the tests no longer redirect recovery evidence outside the device lock. This normal lifecycle completion does not adopt abandoned journals or clear quarantine. Deterministic tests cover install/permission/launch/stop/restore/remove ordering, stale logs, invalid endpoints, lost replies, changed artifacts, multiple users, mapping ownership and real lease cleanup. Actual host inspection passed for the maintained Debug APK. Managed CLI/Worker runs now connect supervised builds and installed-app launch; independent child installation/process/permission-set receipts are connected for ordinary attempts, while nonempty permission scenarios and actual Android platform business acceptance remain unfinished; high-level device permission declarations are still rejected.'],
  ['mock-rule-set-pipeline', 'Shared and Item mock rule selection', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/mocks/MockRuleSet.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/MockManager.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/mocks/MockRuntime.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/Scenario.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RecordingConfig.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/MockRuleSet.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/mock-rule-sets.test.ts'], 'Named sources explicitly enumerate existing endpoint JSON files in project-shared or Item-local directories. Selections qualify method/path/rule by source; replacing a previous selection requires replace: true and replaces the complete response. Sources and rules are validated before App mutation; unselected endpoints are not activated. Parent and child source layers compose into one strict Flutter application before auth/navigation. Every selected attempt, including retry, replaces all routes, call history and the previous rule catalog, denies unmatched traffic by default, verifies active route identities and clears partial routes on failure. Runtime reports contain selected source/file/rule provenance and SHA-256 content hashes without response bodies. Vitest persists mockRuleSet metadata for explicit and default scenarios; declaration fingerprints remain separate from resolved content fingerprints. These deterministic runtime and runner tests do not establish real-device or backend integration acceptance.'],
  ['managed-native-preparation-pipeline', 'Retained native owner preparation between managed launches', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedNativeRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedAppRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/finite.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/commands/run.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-control-env.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskManagedApp.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/managed-app-fixtures.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-native.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-app.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-native-launch.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerNativeOwnership.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/NativePreparationIpc.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/native-ownership-ipc.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/processes.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/worker.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/native-preparation-ipc.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/native-ownership-ipc.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/worker-native-ownership.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/processes.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/executor-timeline.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/NativeSession.test.ts'], 'CLI and Worker establish a private control channel without a bootstrap app or endpoint when native automation is enabled. runCommand defers initial VM discovery only for explicit validated control and owner delegation in managed-start mode with native enabled; inherited credentials cannot enable this path. The runner clears both VM URL environment aliases, and Vitest requests each attempt endpoint before driver connection. Static skips perform no launch, and ordinary Flutter/attach runs keep initial acquisition. ManagedAppRun calls the retained device owner after old managed-process cleanup and before launch/endpoint verification for every native-enabled managed attempt. Worker disposes the previous endpoint lease before a successor and the latest lease on success or runner failure; launch/verification failure fences later preparation without replay. ManagedNativeRun validates a strict single-use attempt identity and paired scenario name/fingerprint against its frozen owner configuration, rejecting concurrent, repeated, failed or closed preparation. Device scenarios create, probe and dispose a fresh preparation Adapter; ordinary and app-only peers use the callback without creating an Adapter or falling back to defaultScenario. The child independently checks readiness after relaunch; supervisor replies are not preparation receipts. Local CLI calls the retained owner directly. Worker executor sends only project/attempt/scenario identity over its private supervisor IPC; NativePreparationIpc checks the current checkout project, field/length limits, one active operation, replay and a 120-second budget. Cancellation sends a separate request ID; timeout, disconnection and failure fence further preparation and never replay requests. Underlying work remains active until it really settles even after an early failure reply. WorkerNativeOwnership owns the platform execution, cancels and waits before final reset, refuses reset/reuse when preparation remains unresolved after 15 seconds, and preserves reset and release errors. Adapter/Runner construction also uses the cross-process device operation mutex, preventing platform activity before the first Adapter request from crossing supervisor preparation. Real Node executor tests verify actual cancellation after dispatch and refusal of later work; direct and supervised executor tests cover multiple attempts. This is read-only required-capability preparation: destructive permission, baseline, biometric, media and camera declarations remain rejected. Installed Android ordinary attempts additionally prepare an empty permission declaration and require independent child installation/process/permission-set readback. Nonempty declaration integration and actual mobile business acceptance remain unfinished.'],
  ['android-permission-preparation-pipeline', 'Android permission-manager preparation primitive', ['packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidProcessInventory.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidPermissionPreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeBinding.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidPermissionPreparation.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidManagedApp.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidAppObservation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AndroidRecoveryPlatform.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/android/AndroidManagedApp.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/NativeDeviceSession.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/NativeSession.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceLeaseOperation.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/DeviceLeaseDelegation.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/ScenarioDevicePreparation.ts'], 'The retained NativeDeviceSession owner exposes low-level preparePermissions for camera/microphone on a dedicated Android emulator in managed-start mode. Its stopApp primitive validates the installed package, primary user and isolated UID before one force-stop dispatch, then confirms the stopped flag and absence of all UID processes; an acknowledgement alone is insufficient. Permission preparation, installed-app launch/cleanup, child readback and recovery share one bounded numeric process-inventory parser: records need safe integer UIDs/PIDs, unique PIDs, exactly UID/PID/name columns and root PID 1. Numeric UID comparison catches surviving custom processes even with a padded UID. Invalid inventories cannot authorize later permission/forward/uninstall writes; failed final stop readback retains the lease quarantine and original recovery evidence. Its independent inspectPermissions reads grants/flags and default app-op policy while the app or Adapter may run, performs no writes and never adopts a restoration baseline. Stopping or permission mutation requires the local Adapter to be disposed; observation serializes with Adapter work. Permission mutation additionally requires targetSdk >= 23, a force-stopped target and no process of that UID, including custom process names; read-only observation does not require app stop. The entire batch and original grants/flags are validated before mutation; fixed, policy, one-time and unknown flags are refused. Only default package app-op policy is accepted; custom or UID-wide modes are refused and app-op modes are never changed. Prompt revokes and clears USER_SET/USER_FIXED; denied revokes and sets USER_SET while clearing USER_FIXED, expressing prior denial rather than permanent denial; granted grants and clears those flags. Recognized user-sensitive flags are preserved. Real pm commands are followed by grant/flags/app-op readback. Each declaration replaces the previous one and omitted permissions return to captured originals. Owner disposal restores originals under one independent cleanup deadline shared with outstanding work and Adapter disposal, after final app stop; an owned installation is independently verified and stopped by owner quiescence, while permission-only callers must stop their own app. Restoration does not restart the budget. Deadline expiry aborts the same signal, so a delayed read or mutex wait cannot dispatch later restoration writes. Already-dispatched work can remain unknown and retains quarantine. Quiescence stops only a verified owned installation and retains device exclusion through Worker reset; disposal then releases ownership. Dispatched permission writes and force-stop are marked unknown until full readback; failed, cancelled or unconfirmed writes are never replayed or blindly restored and quarantine the device. Schema-cloned config prevents later caller mutation from redirecting device, package or tool paths. Parent preparation, delegated Adapter requests and VM process binding share a cross-process operation mutex without reacquiring the delegated task claim. Lock waits consume call budgets; unresolved platform work retains exclusion and release refuses active work. Initialization, cleanup, quarantine and release errors preserve their causes. Deterministic and real-child ownership regressions cover these contracts. No actual Android permission business acceptance has passed: scenario permissions and device.permissions.set remain rejected, and no high-level capability is promoted by these tests. Modern Android app-op behavior and the managed CLI/Worker preparation/relaunch integration remain pending platform validation.'],
  ['managed-app-attempt-pipeline', 'Supervised managed app attempts', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/ManagedAppLifecycle.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/ScenarioDevicePreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/ManagedAppLifecycle.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedAppRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/finite.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/commands/run.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-app.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/tests/managed-control-env.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskManagedApp.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskEndpointLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/AttemptContext.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/runner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/managed-app-fixtures.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-tdd/src/daemon/SubprocessDaemonTransport.ts', 'packages/fliwright-tdd/tests/daemon/SubprocessDaemonTransport.cleanup.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/executor-launch.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/executor-timeline.test.ts'], 'Native-enabled managed attempts use one authenticated loopback supervisor channel with private environment credentials, strict session/protocol/body validation, single-use attempt keys and bounded preparation/cleanup. Scenario name and full fingerprint are paired and verified against supervisor configuration before stopping the app; attempts without a scenario are supported. Every attempt, including retry, ordinary peers and app-only scenarios, requests a fresh stop/launch/verified endpoint. Retaining an initial endpoint after a previous relaunch is prohibited. Vitest retains task device ownership, disposes old attempt resources and releases only the old endpoint before requesting relaunch. It binds the new endpoint to the current attempt, creates an attempt-specific driver and rechecks VM/device process identity. Managed driver cleanup follows passive diagnosis inside finalization; unknown cleanup fails even a skipped attempt, quarantines the device and prevents subsequent launches. Duplicate, concurrent and failed/unknown requests cannot replay writes. Client cancellation reaches owned startup; disposal fails if preparation remains unresolved. CLI and Worker create new transports per launch; process disposal confirms exit after SIGTERM/SIGKILL rather than trusting delivery. Worker supervisor device ownership still outlives executor termination and final reset. Credentials are absent from shared configuration, job journals and reports, and inherited control is cleared without an explicit supervisor. Subprocess and real-worker regressions cover the lifecycle. A real iOS 26.5 managed CLI run now passes consecutive native confirmation/cancellation cases with separate managed-app and Adapter generations and current VM PID binding; Android relaunch and the complete mobile acceptance matrix remain pending. Device permission/baseline/media/camera mutations remain rejected before platform work until preparation/readback/restoration is implemented. Attach and native-disabled runs do not use this relaunch channel.'],
  ['camera-test-seam-pipeline', 'App-owned camera result automation', ['packages/fliwright-bridge/lib/src/testing/camera_test.dart', 'packages/fliwright-bridge/test/camera_test_test.dart', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/CameraTestController.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/tests/CameraTestController.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/tests/camera-fixtures.test.ts', 'examples/form_demo/lib/camera_fixture_main.dart', 'examples/form_demo/lib/camera_fixture.dart', 'examples/form_demo/test/camera_fixture_test.dart', 'e2e/.fliwright/support/camera-scenarios.ts', 'e2e/.fliwright/tests/camera.test.ts'], 'The dedicated app test entrypoint explicitly registers FliwrightCameraTestCapability with app-readable fixture loaders and injects capture into its camera service. Release registration is refused; production plugins are never replaced automatically. Image, cancellation and error results are queued once per capture; empty queues fail. Fixture IDs are portable references, PNG/JPEG bytes must decode, and loaders have a five-second budget, a 10 MB byte limit and a 16 MP decoded image limit. Each generation accepts at most 32 results. Generation checks fence delayed captures, loaders and old cleanup. CameraTestController validates the app status protocol, serializes requests with a default five-second bound and refuses further enqueue after an unknown write outcome. The lazy Vitest cameraTest fixture starts each attempt with a new generation, checks leftovers and resets using the old generation during bounded finalization. Unconsumed results fail even a skipped body; a real-worker regression verifies retry isolation, body-error preservation, skip cleanup and the following peer. Cleanup cancellation prevents subsequent requests. RunSession operations use surface flutter, coverage app-test-seam and testSeam camera; consumption metadata records counts without image bytes or host paths. The form_demo test entrypoint consumes only public bridge APIs. Its named camera.empty scenario and portable E2E test cover preview, cancellation retaining the prior image, an error message, retake and a local upload receiver. This path has passed real Flutter CLI runs on macOS and iOS Simulator 26.5. The iOS run consumes the same four results and retains app-test-seam metadata; an earlier incomplete-host staging failure is preserved separately. These runs do not establish Android mobile acceptance, permission UI, native camera UI, network/backend upload or physical/virtual camera coverage. No native Adapter is required for this seam.'],
  ['native-android-mixed-fixture-pipeline', 'Maintained Flutter and native Android fixture', ['examples/form_demo/native_fixtures/android/MainActivity.java', 'examples/form_demo/lib/native_fixture.dart', 'examples/form_demo/lib/native_fixture_main.dart', 'examples/form_demo/test/native_fixture_test.dart', 'scripts/prepare-native-demo.mjs', 'scripts/tests/prepare-native-demo.test.mjs', 'scripts/native-stack-contract.mjs', 'scripts/tests/native-stack-contract.test.mjs', 'harness/stack/framework.json', 'e2e/native.android.project.ts', 'e2e/.fliwright/tests/native-android-mixed.test.ts'], 'prepare-native-demo accepts an explicit android platform and creates a fresh disposable Java Flutter host outside the repository. It stages the maintained Activity, CAMERA/RECORD_AUDIO manifest permissions, shared Dart screen, Android E2E tests and workspace configuration without device operations. The Activity registers its channel only when the app is debuggable. It opens a real AlertDialog and requests real runtime permissions; results are read back with checkSelfPermission and incomplete callbacks fail instead of inventing denial. Pending requests fail on Activity destruction. Android-only nativeDemo.readPermissions independently returns checkSelfPermission and shouldShowRequestPermissionRationale without changing permissions; false rationale is not a prompt/permanent-denial classification. Dart validates the full observation shape and exposes the camera action only in the Android fixture. The named native.ready scenario resets app UI only. Six E2E cases cover confirmation/cancellation and camera/microphone allow/deny with Flutter assertions plus app readback, but Android device business acceptance is still pending. Permission cases require independent externally prepared prompt baselines and permission-controller resource IDs verified on the selected OS. Stack checks govern Java/Kotlin sources and imports, including static/wildcard imports, and reject Gradle projects/dependency manifests inside source fixtures. These fixtures do not enable managed permission scenarios, prove automatic restoration, microphone recording, biometric events or camera frames.'],
  ['native-ios-mixed-fixture-pipeline', 'Maintained Flutter and native iOS smoke fixture', ['examples/form_demo/lib/native_fixture_main.dart', 'examples/form_demo/lib/native_fixture.dart', 'examples/form_demo/native_fixtures/ios/AppDelegate.swift', 'examples/form_demo/test/native_fixture_test.dart', 'scripts/prepare-native-demo.mjs', 'scripts/tests/prepare-native-demo.test.mjs', 'scripts/native-stack-contract.mjs', 'scripts/tests/native-stack-contract.test.mjs', 'harness/stack/framework.json', 'e2e/native.project.ts', 'e2e/.fliwright/support/native-scenarios.ts', 'e2e/.fliwright/tests/native-mixed.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeBinding.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/tests/NativeBinding.test.ts'], 'The maintained Dart entrypoint registers nativeDemo.reset and displays the actual result from a Flutter MethodChannel. Its source-only Swift overlay presents a real UIKit confirmation/cancellation and calls the real microphone permission API; it never supplies permission decisions itself. prepare-native-demo generates a fresh disposable iOS Flutter host outside the checkout, stages owned sources and workspace dependencies, adds the microphone usage description and explicitly enables FLIWRIGHT_NATIVE_FIXTURE in Debug.xcconfig. Channel registration also requires targetEnvironment(simulator); generated Flutter Runner Debug does not implicitly define Swift DEBUG. Stack governance validates the source roster/framework imports and refuses generated Xcode projects or dependency/build manifests in maintained source fixtures. Four TypeScript smoke cases consume public page/native/expect fixtures and a named native.ready scenario. Real iOS 26.5/Xcode 26.6 managed CLI runs pass UIKit confirm/cancel in app scope and microphone Allow/Don’t Allow in SpringBoard system scope, each followed by a Flutter text assertion. Consecutive confirmation cases use independent managed app and Adapter generations; VM binding validates the current PID against the exact observed UIKitApplication bundle label. Microphone cases run separately with externally reset prompt baselines, not high-level permission declarations. The original binding/channel failures remain in local evidence. These smoke passes do not establish automatic permission restoration, Android, microphone recording, biometrics, camera frames, physical hardware or the required 20-attempt reliability matrix. Temporary generated hosts and device configuration are not repository source.'],
  ['native-session-pipeline', 'Native device session foundation', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/native/NativeRuntime.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/native/NativeLocator.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RecordingConfig.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/ScenarioDevicePreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskScenarioPreparation.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDeviceSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeBinding.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/native/NativeEvidence.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/TaskNativeSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/runner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/native.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/NativeDoctor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/DeviceLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/ManagedNativeRun.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerNativeOwnership.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/native-ownership-ipc.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/android/AdbSession.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/ios/PrepareIosRunner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/ios/XctestRunner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/src/ios/RunnerBroker.ts', 'packages/fliwright-native/runners/ios/FliwrightRunner.swift'], 'Explicit createNativeSession configuration selects one online device and acquires a host-wide device lease before constructing ADB or XCTest adapters. Core owns strict fresh locators, native assertions, deadlines, cancellation and optional RunSession operation recording; native owns platform processes, bounded XML/wire parsing and cleanup/quarantine. A cancelled or unfinished operation prevents reuse, and unknown writes are never replayed. iOS Runner preparation is explicit and caches unsigned Simulator build products by source/protocol/toolchain/architecture inputs. XCTest attaches without launching the target; locator filters are pushed down and host scope/matches are rechecked. Shared config transport accepts the optional native section. Vitest native/device fixtures and native expect share task ownership across retries, bind the current VM PID to the selected device, and clean attempts on skip, timeout or failure. RunSession surface providers capture native evidence directly without recursively entering its action queue; native trees retain their own wrapper and surface metadata through offline export and Cloud/VS Code viewers. CLI native doctor is read-only, snapshot owns a temporary platform session, and prepare explicitly builds the Simulator Runner. Managed CLI obtains ownership before Flutter launch and passes a private environment grant to test children. The Cloud Worker supervisor owns the device across executor termination and final reset using bounded IPC delegation; child task claims serialize within the parent owner, and lost ownership fences operations and stops adapters. Device scenario declarations validate portable references and singleton inheritance. Read-only requiredCapabilities preparation runs before managed launch and Bridge connection, repeats for every retry, and hands the app stage a single-use live receipt bound to the full scenario fingerprint, attempt, mode and Adapter generation. The app stage rechecks readiness and records a device summary. Preparation reads retain queue/deadline/lease controls without starting RunSession operations before recording fixtures exist. Worker direct runs explicitly disable repository default scenarios. Destructive baseline/permission/media/camera declarations and app-only scenario.apply/reset are refused before platform/Bridge work. Managed app stop/relaunch/verified endpoint handoff is implemented separately through ManagedAppLifecycle; every native-enabled managed attempt uses a fresh endpoint and VM binding. A maintained iOS Simulator fixture now passes real Flutter-to-UIKit confirmation/cancellation and system microphone Allow/Don’t Allow flows back to Flutter assertions. NativeBinding accepts the observed iOS 26 UIKitApplication label using whole-label parsing and exact bundle/PID equality. Permission prompts are reset externally between microphone cases; destructive device preparation and the complete mixed acceptance matrix remain unfinished. iOS fill/biometrics remain unverified; high-level permission declarations, media import and camera sources are not enabled. The Android retained owner separately implements low-level permission preparation with durable parent-owned recovery evidence, but actual mobile permission acceptance remains pending. Android fingerprint event capability remains unverified until device-bound enrollment, challenge and baseline recovery are proven. Candidate IDs do not certify readiness; the baselineVerified boolean is rejected. The separate biometricTest observer tracks app-local callbacks and never establishes hardware support itself.'],
  ['worker-service-diagnostics-pipeline', 'Worker service monitoring and offline diagnostics', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/worker-diagnostics.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/diagnostics.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/main.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/host.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/worker.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/WorkerDiagnosticsStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/WorkerDiagnostics.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/VmServiceEndpoint.ts'], 'Worker service sessions are independent of test leases. Private, redacted JSONL files rotate locally and retain startup, component, execution and fatal diagnostics; the offline logs command reads the latest 100 records. A separate outbound monitoring connection sends state, PID, Node version, memory, uptime, event-loop lag and acknowledged log batches every 10 seconds, replaying retained records after reconnect. Cloud deduplicates by authenticated Worker/session/sequence, keeps bounded SQLite history and marks missing service heartbeats unreachable after 35 seconds. Operators and administrators browse, filter, page and download the current diagnostic query from Worker hosts; simultaneous active sessions are flagged. Background component failures are supervised immediately; unhandled exceptions/rejections log synchronously and exit nonzero. SIGKILL cannot log its cause, so the next start reports missing clean shutdown and OS/supervisor logs remain necessary. Protocol incompatibility is diagnosed with client and bridge versions and preserved through durable Worker timeline recording and Cloud normalization.'],
  ['cloud-flutter-launch-pipeline', 'Cloud Flutter app launch from workspace entries', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/contracts.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/tests/executor-launch.test.ts'], 'Project projectPath anchors repository installation, test configuration, test selection and artifact source capture. The configured Flutter entry is resolved relative to that directory. Before spawning Flutter, the Worker locates the nearest ancestor pubspec.yaml of the entry inside the configured project, runs flutter run --machine from that package directory, and rebases the -t argument to the package. This supports workspace-root tests with nested Flutter applications and preserves standalone application launches. Missing entries, directory entries, entries outside the configured project and entries without an owning package are rejected before spawning Flutter. Device selection and Flutter arguments are passed to the owned machine process; VM Service acquisition and bridge verification precede test execution.'],
  ['cloud-scenario-builder-pipeline', 'Cloud scenario creation and Mock composition', ['packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Projects.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/ScenarioBuilder.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/scenario-builder.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/catalog.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/contracts.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/catalog-scanner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/Scenario.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RecordingConfig.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/tests/scenario-builder-ui.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/tests/scenario-builder.test.ts'], 'Scenarios are optional: direct runs omit scenario selection and do not automatically load Mock rules. The project view selector opens scenario management and its three-step builder before project readiness. Users select a Mock directory, optional profile and starting route, then choose individual response overrides from scanned Mock directories. Login, guest, expired-session and user-specific states are expressed by Mock profiles rather than a direct auth setting. Collection publishes profile candidates, endpoint/method/rule names and default selections without response bodies, discovering the default directory and directories declared in existing scenarios. The builder pins the selected catalog while editing, validates unique names and rule choices, previews/copies/downloads a standalone typed scenario file and provides an import/merge snippet for the existing repository configuration. It does not change readiness or write Git; users commit, push, rescan and obtain administrator readiness confirmation. Runtime Mock layers load defaults once, optionally apply a shared profile, then apply validated method-specific endpoint selections before navigation. Missing rules and clipboard/download failures retain a usable preview.'],
  ['cloud-projects-ui-pipeline', 'Cloud project browsing and dialog workflows', ['packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Projects.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/app.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/components/ui/dialog.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/project-setup.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/tests/projects.test.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/tests/web-routing.test.ts'], 'Cloud opens the execution board at / and retains /queue as its direct navigation entry. The previous operational overview lives at /overview in the configuration menu. Cloud uses a single top navigation row with direct board and test-case entries. The searchable project switcher sits before these links on project routes. The case workspace starts with its view title and actions, followed by a full-width suite-grouped table showing case names, files, latest run status and time, and run actions. The case table scrolls independently within the viewport while its column headings, catalog selector and search remain visible; settings and other project views retain vertical scrolling. Narrow screens keep run actions visible and omit the time column. The existing semantic theme and light/dark preference remain unchanged. Project selection is driven by /projects/$projectId; opening /projects selects the first project with a history replace. Project-switcher links preserve the workspace while browser back/forward and direct URLs restore the selected project. Unknown project IDs show a missing-project state instead of falling back to another project, and nested project pages keep the test-case navigation active. Administrators open the same creation dialog from the page heading or from the global Submit test dropdown. The dropdown retains test-submission navigation; its project-creation link uses a one-time search flag consumed without replacing the app shell. An accessible page-view select keeps test cases, scenarios, run history and settings reachable without adding a second navigation bar. Project status summaries live in settings, alongside repository configuration, readiness and automatic-run management. Mobile global search opens as a dismissible overlay, while submission and theme controls remain available in the configuration menu. Cloud uses shared shadcn/radix-mira primitives and Tailwind utilities for page, board, replay and timeline layouts. The CSS entry only imports Tailwind and the semantic theme; business selector rules are absent. Only ready catalogs matching the current configuration revision are selectable. Case and suite actions open a confirmation dialog pinned to the selected catalog and default to direct execution without scenario preparation or automatically loaded Mock rules, and optionally expose repository-discovered scenarios; successful submission opens run history. Repository scans choose the default branch or a validated full commit SHA, then show filterable scan history with expandable failures and cancellation for administrators and operators. Creation, editing and automatic-run configuration use focused dialogs, while deletion requires the exact project name. Members can browse and run ready tests but cannot edit project settings or scan repositories. Shared shadcn/radix-mira Dialog primitives contain focus, lock background scrolling and restore the triggering control on dismissal; pending submissions prevent dismissal. Failed saves retain their input and show errors within the dialog. Project refresh preserves the automatic-run dialog and its one-time webhook token.'],
  ['cloud-worker-auto-binding-pipeline', 'Automatic Worker device binding and executor synchronization', ['packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Devices.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/CloudStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/worker-control.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/config.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/main.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/host.ts'], 'A paired host runs once with its minimal credential configuration. Selecting a device application enrolls and enables it; Cloud allocates a stable slot when its ID is omitted and repairs existing slotless enrollments during host assignment synchronization. The authenticated assignments operation returns only that host’s managed, enabled, fresh devices. The host follows pushed configuration changes, creates private per-slot configuration and runtime directories, and owns an independent control connection per executor. Disabling or removing a binding stops its executor. iOS simulators boot automatically and use shutdown, boot and bootstatus before and after execution, preserving data without erase. Other device platforms require local resetCommands. Managed application bindings also grant catalog scanning scope without editing server configuration. Catalog collection remains independent of device leases; quarantined slots and retained locks require explicit recovery.'],
  ['worker-execution-timeline-pipeline', 'Worker execution timeline', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/worker-timeline.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerTimelineRecorder.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerTimelineChannel.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/ManagedNativeTimeline.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/worker-timeline-ipc.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerAgent.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/executor.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/WorkerTimelineStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/WorkerExecutionTimeline.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/app.tsx'], 'Each Cloud ExecutionAttempt owns an independent Worker timeline for environment preparation, app launch, test runner execution, report publication and device cleanup. Event schema v2 adds numbered parent spans for managed native app attempts; supervisor-only hashed attempt keys associate build/install/stop/prepare/launch/verify children without persisting the key, while readers retain version 1 compatibility. The supervisor assigns monotonic sequence numbers and timestamps, persists events to a private local spool before upload, and sends batches over the authenticated Worker control WebSocket. Cloud validates lease fencing and span order, stores events independently from immutable test evidence, and exposes a paginated read model. Task details show attempt groups and their phase durations; reports resolve the exact execution that published them. Test Replay and its test-only timeline remain unchanged, and shared reports do not request Worker data.'],
  ['cloud-worker-control-pipeline', 'Outbound WebSocket Worker control and HTTPS report publication', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/worker-control.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/WorkerControlClient.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/worker-control.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/CloudStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/main.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/WorkerAgent.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/catalog-scanner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/worker.ts'], 'Workers initiate a Bearer-authenticated /api/worker/connect WebSocket upgrade, using WSS for HTTPS servers. A strict operation allowlist and request IDs carry device inventory, claims, scan results, execution heartbeats, outcomes and recovery. Committed scheduling changes wake idle Workers; no periodic task or scan claim requests are sent. Cancellation is pushed to the owning Worker. Transport ping/pong maintains connection liveness and idle executor presence, while execution and scan heartbeats independently fence 30-second leases. Disconnect rejects pending RPCs without replay, aborts owned execution/scans, and uses exponential reconnect backoff. Interrupted device slots retain local locks and require explicit recovery. Report publication creation, binary files and commit use the HTTP data plane. Server and Worker must upgrade together; old HTTP control routes and Worker pollMs are removed.'],
  ['cloud-worker-pairing-pipeline', 'One-time Worker enrollment and credential revocation', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/worker-pairing.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/CloudStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/WorkerPairing.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/pairing.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/config.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/main.ts'], 'An administrator creates a ten-minute single-use code in the Workers page. Cloud stores only its digest; the Worker enters it interactively over HTTPS and atomically redeems it for a per-host credential, registered without static server configuration or restart. The CLI reserves a new private directory and writes 0600 credentials and host config, never printing the code or token. Cloud stores only the long-lived credential digest; it authenticates WSS and report publication, and is limited to Worker operations. Admins can inspect status and revoke pending codes or paired credentials; revocation terminates active WSS connections. A single run command starts host discovery, catalog collection and automatic executor binding; device enrollment in Cloud selects the application scope.'],
  ['cloud-replay-pipeline', 'Cloud report replay and screenshot timeline', ['packages/fliwright-cloud/src/ReportStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Replay.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/replay-model.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/TimeTimeline.tsx'], 'Published reports provide archived timeline nodes and artifact paths. Replay keeps steps in an independent resizable left panel and places the timeline above the screenshot preview and information panels on the right; narrow screens stack these regions. Thin action lines retain recorded start times, durations and overlap lanes, with accessible labels and native hover titles. Playback controls live below the timeline; its ruler handle and vertical playhead share the elapsed-time scale, replacing the separate seek bar. Playback advances linearly with requestAnimationFrame and recorded wall-clock durations at 0.5x, 1x or 2x speed. Pause and speed changes retain the current time, dragging pauses playback, completion stops at the exact endpoint, and replay from the endpoint restarts at zero. The zoomed viewport follows the playhead horizontally; the preview updates only when playback selects a different operation. The screenshot filmstrip shares the same elapsed scale and zoom. Images are anchored to step completion, falling back to step start for unfinished actions because archived screenshot references do not carry capture timestamps. Nearby thumbnails use separate rows and endpoint images remain inside the surface. Selecting a thumbnail chooses its step and image index in the preview; selecting a step scrolls its screenshot into view. Only archived image paths are resolved, duplicate failure references are removed, shared reports use their authorized file endpoint, and failed image loads show a placeholder.'],
  ['cloud-test-board-pipeline', 'Cloud test status Kanban and on-demand details', ['packages/fliwright-cloud/src/server.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/CloudStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/app.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/TestBoard.tsx', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Replay.tsx'], 'The overview, queue, run history and report listings share a paginated Kanban. Queued requests enter pending; assigned, preparing, running, finalizing and cancellation-in-progress enter testing. Only completed passed verdicts enter success. Completed failed verdicts and infrastructure failures enter failure; cancellations, skipped, interrupted and unknown completed verdicts appear under other results. The queue and history fetch all lifecycle states, while reports retain the completed-state filter. Cards show scenario, suite, application, commit, branch, actor and submission time. Pending cards follow queue position. Search and application filters reset pagination; column counts explicitly describe the current page. Listings poll every three seconds and move cards by lifecycle and verdict. Clicking a card opens the request route, which preserves live diagnostics and redirects to a published report when available. Details and event streams are loaded only after opening a card. The board fills the available page width and viewport height, with independently scrolling lifecycle columns. Narrow screens scroll the board horizontally beneath the same single-row header.'],
  ['device-discovery-pipeline', 'Host device discovery and cloud inventory', ['packages/fliwright-cloud-protocol/src/contracts.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/main.ts', 'packages/fliwright-devices/src/DeviceDiscovery.ts', 'packages/fliwright-devices/src/device-changes.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/registry/devices.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud-worker/src/device-reporting.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/CloudStore.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/device-events.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/data.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cloud/src/web/Devices.tsx'], 'The shared devices package normalizes native Android and Xcode inventories without an app connection. CLI exposes devices list. Cloud Workers scan at startup and WS reconnect, and native adb track-devices, xcdevice observe and simulator state-file events trigger coalesced rescans and serialized WS reports. Native observers restart after failures; periodic 30-second reconciliation renews freshness and covers incomplete native notifications. Browser-only /api/device-events SSE invalidates device list/detail reads on committed changes and time-based readiness transitions, replacing three-second browser polling. Reconnect invalidates missed events, subscriptions reauthenticate every minute, and live refresh preserves unsaved settings edits. The server marks snapshots stale by receipt time and the browser distinguishes connection state from configured slot state. Discovery never releases leases. The cloud registry preserves host-scoped device settings and AVD identity across scans; operator enrollment configures application scope and slots. Scheduling requires fresh online discovery and matching runtime device identity. /devices provides filtering, details and persistent policy management; /workers retains host health and diagnostics.'],
  ['cli-workflow-pipeline', 'Skill, CLI and CI', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/CommandRegistry.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/session/ConnectionHost.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/commands/run.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/runner.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RunSession.ts'], 'The registry owns discovery and validation. Finite runs collect Vitest outcomes and canonical evidence. Connection workers retain reusable objects and serialize commands; they do not replace RunSession or its journals.'],
  ['recording-pipeline', 'Recording and generation', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/commands/record.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RecordingCapture.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/RecorderController.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/EventAggregator.ts', 'packages/fliwright-cli/src/RecordingReplay.ts'], 'Capture owns start/stop and flushing. Code generation produces drafts; readiness and unchanged-candidate replay evidence determine whether tests may be published. The recording endpoint lease releases before replay forks start.'],
  ['self-healing-pipeline', 'Assertion and self-healing', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/Assertion.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/SelfHealingEngine.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/SnapshotStore.ts'], 'Assertions gather failure context and optional repair suggestions. Stable selectors belong in durable tests; screenshots, timelines and source context preserve failure evidence.'],
  ['form-fill-pipeline', 'Form autofill', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/FormHelper.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/SemanticInferrer.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/FakerGenerator.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/SelectorResolver.ts'], 'Extract fields, infer semantics, resolve rules and generate values, then interact through locators.'],
  ['scenario-lease-pipeline', 'Scenarios and endpoint ownership', ['packages/fliwright-core/src/Scenario.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/EndpointLease.ts', 'packages/fliwright-vitest/src/index.ts', 'packages/fliwright-tdd/src/runtime/TddRuntime.ts'], 'Fixtures acquire endpoint ownership and apply named composed scenarios before each attempt, including retries. TDD releases control-phase ownership before rerun forks acquire their leases.'],
  ['vm-service-endpoint-pipeline', 'VM Service endpoint acquisition', ['packages/fliwright-cli/src/vm-discovery.ts', 'packages/fliwright-core/src/VMServiceConnector.ts', 'packages/fliwright-tdd/src/daemon/FlutterDaemonEndpointSource.ts'], 'Explicit CLI inputs, project/environment configuration, discovery or managed Flutter launches resolve the endpoint. VM transport selects the runnable isolate; ref tokens belong to the app/isolate incarnation, so transport reconnect alone does not invalidate them.'],
];
for (const [file, title, trace, description] of pipelines) {
  for (const source of trace) if (!exists(source)) throw new Error(`Pipeline source moved: ${source}`);
  document(`${file}.md`, { feature: title, status: 'implemented', agent_accessible: true, source: trace }, `# ${title}\n\n${description}\n\n## Implementation trace\n\n${trace.map((source, index) => `${index + 1}. [${source}](/${path.resolve(source).slice(1)})`).join('\n')}\n\n## Agent access\n\nUse the current [CLI command reference](./cli/README.md) and project-local Skills. Preserve canonical run evidence for offline investigation.`);
}
document('index.md', { purpose: 'AI-agent-consumable feature index' }, `# Fliwright feature index\n\nGenerated from workspace package manifests, public source exports, command schemas, Dart barrels and VM extension registrations.\n\n## By Package\n\n| Package | Responsibility | Overview | Detailed Docs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n${packages.map((item) => `| ${item.name} | ${item.description} | [${item.slug}](./${item.slug}/README.md) | ${item.modules.length} modules |`).join('\n')}\n\n## By Class\n\n| Class | Package | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n${indexClasses.map((item) => `| ${item.title} | ${item.package} | [${item.title}](./${item.slug}/${item.doc}) |`).join('\n')}\n\n## By Feature Slice\n\n| Feature | Reference | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n${pipelines.map(([file, title]) => `| ${title} | [Pipeline](./${file}.md) | Implemented |`).join('\n')}\n\n## CLI Command Quick Reference\n\n| Command | Input fields | Reference |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n${commands.map((item) => `| ${item.path} | ${item.input} | [Schema](./${item.doc}) |`).join('\n')}\n\n## Quick Start for AI Agents\n\n1. Discover exact command input with \`fliwright schema <path> --json\`.\n2. Inspect the app with \`app snapshot\`, \`app observe\` and \`app debug-snapshot\`.\n3. Run tests with \`run --json\` (optionally \`--scenario <name>\`); retain run artifacts and optional JUnit output in CI.\n4. Diagnose offline with \`artifacts failures\`, \`artifacts timeline\` and \`artifacts show\`.\n5. Generate or record drafts with \`generate-test\` and \`record --duration\`; inspect readiness before accepting tests.\n6. Open an explicit connection session for persistent TDD and close it when finished.\n7. Prepare app-owned state through named scenarios; use app-owned auth seeding for business tests.\n8. Use \`flow\` commands for runtime/Figma review and report generation.`);
// Prepare the entire output in memory before replacing the generated directory.
fs.rmSync(output, { recursive: true, force: true });
for (const [file, content] of pending) { const target = path.join(output, file); fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(target), { recursive: true }); fs.writeFileSync(target, content); }
console.log(`Generated ${pending.size} implementation references for ${packages.length} packages and ${commands.length} CLI commands.`);
